From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000004 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Philippe Simons <simons.philippe@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 14:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] fix: make the legacy GAMESCOPE_FPS_LIMIT atom actually stick

The QuickAccess frame-rate cap had no effect on HW. Localised 2026-07-25 on the
device: the Steam client writes the chosen cap into the legacy X root atom
`GAMESCOPE_FPS_LIMIT` correctly (verified: `xprop -root` reads 30 after picking
30 in SteamUI), but gamescope forgets it again within one frame.

`gamescopeFPSLimit`'s property handler assigns `g_nSteamCompMgrTargetFPS`
directly. That global is not the source of truth: `paint_all()` calls
`update_app_target_refresh_cycle()` on EVERY frame, and
`_update_app_target_refresh_cycle()` unconditionally resets
`g_nSteamCompMgrTargetFPS = 0` before recomputing it from
`g_nCombinedAppRefreshCycleOverride[]` — which is written only by the
`gamescope_control` Wayland protocol (`set_app_target_refresh_cycle`) and the
`debug_set_fps_limit` console command. So the atom's value survives at most one
vblank and the pacing condition at `steamcompmgr_should_vblank_window()` never
sees it. This is the same in 3.16.17, so it is not a regression from our version
bump — upstream simply never exercises the atom path, because Valve's x86 client
drives the cap over the Wayland protocol instead.

Our arm64 Steam client does NOT do that: it never opens gamescope's Wayland
socket at all (HW-checked with `lsof -U`: only the two Xwayland servers are
connected), so the legacy atom is the only channel it has. Route it through the
same override the protocol writes, so it persists.

`change_refresh=false`: refresh switching stays owned by
`GAMESCOPE_DYNAMIC_REFRESH` / the control protocol; this patch only restores the
FPS cap. `update_runtime_info()` is dropped here because
`steamcompmgr_set_app_refresh_cycle_override()` already runs it via
`update_app_target_refresh_cycle()` — that is what flips the
`GAMESCOPE_LIMITER_FILE` flag the WSI layer reads to force FIFO.

Validated on HW before rebuild by bridging the atom to
`gamescopectl debug_set_fps_limit` and timing `vkcube --c 300`: with the value
SteamUI itself wrote, 30 -> 29 fps, 20 -> 20 fps, 0 -> 59 fps.

diff --git a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
--- a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
+++ b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
@@ -6083,7 +6083,18 @@
 	if ( ev->atom == ctx->atoms.gamescopeFPSLimit )
 	{
-		g_nSteamCompMgrTargetFPS = get_prop( ctx, ctx->root, ctx->atoms.gamescopeFPSLimit, 0 );
-		update_runtime_info();
+		// novadeck: route the legacy X-atom cap through the SAME override the
+		// gamescope_control Wayland protocol writes. Assigning
+		// g_nSteamCompMgrTargetFPS directly does not survive: paint_all() calls
+		// update_app_target_refresh_cycle() every frame, which resets the target
+		// to 0 and recomputes it from g_nCombinedAppRefreshCycleOverride[]. Our
+		// arm64 Steam client never connects to gamescope's Wayland socket, so
+		// this atom is the only channel it has for the QuickAccess FPS cap.
+		// change_refresh=false: refresh switching stays with
+		// GAMESCOPE_DYNAMIC_REFRESH / the control protocol.
+		steamcompmgr_set_app_refresh_cycle_override(
+			GetBackend()->GetScreenType(),
+			get_prop( ctx, ctx->root, ctx->atoms.gamescopeFPSLimit, 0 ),
+			false, true );
 	}
 	for (int i = 0; i < gamescope::GAMESCOPE_SCREEN_TYPE_COUNT; i++)
 	{
--
